From: Try Omarchy contributors
Subject: [PATCH] cocoa: re-enable the full grab tap after macOS disables it

Applies after the Try Omarchy full-grab-focus patch.

macOS owns the lifetime of a CGEventTap, not the process that created it.
When a tap's callback returns too slowly the window server disables the tap
and delivers kCGEventTapDisabledByTimeout; a tap can also be switched off
with kCGEventTapDisabledByUserInput. Recovery requires the owner to call
CGEventTapEnable again. setFullGrab never does, and handleTapEvent does not
look at the event type at all, so the first timeout ends system-shortcut
capture for the rest of the session.

Neither notification is a key event, so +[NSEvent eventWithCGEvent:] returns
nil for it and handleEvent reports that it consumed nothing. The tap dies
silently, with no warning and no way back.

The resulting failure is asymmetric, which makes it easy to misread as a
guest problem. Ordinary Command chords keep reaching the guest, because the
preceding patch routes those through key-window focus rather than through
the tap. Only the system shortcuts that need the HID tap to pre-empt macOS
still depend on it, so Command-Space in particular starts opening Spotlight
on the host -- or Siri, which macOS binds to the same chord held down --
and keeps doing so until the VM is restarted.

Re-enable the tap when either notification arrives. This is a recovery path,
not a way to defeat the timeout: if the callback is still too slow the window
server simply disables the tap again.

--- a/ui/cocoa.m
+++ b/ui/cocoa.m
@@ -354,6 +354,7 @@
 - (void) notifyMouseModeChange;
 - (BOOL) isMouseGrabbed;
 - (BOOL) isKeyboardCaptured;
+- (void) reenableEventTap;
 - (void) raiseAllKeys;
 @end
 
@@ -362,6 +363,12 @@
 static CGEventRef handleTapEvent(CGEventTapProxy proxy, CGEventType type, CGEventRef cgEvent, void *userInfo)
 {
     QemuCocoaView *view = userInfo;
+    if (type == kCGEventTapDisabledByTimeout ||
+        type == kCGEventTapDisabledByUserInput) {
+        [view reenableEventTap];
+        return cgEvent;
+    }
+
     NSEvent *event = [NSEvent eventWithCGEvent:cgEvent];
     if (getenv("TRYOMARCHY_INPUT_OBSERVE_ONLY")) {
         [view isKeyboardCaptured];
@@ -1337,6 +1344,14 @@
     return captured;
 }
 
+/* macOS can disable a slow tap; restore capture after either notification. */
+- (void) reenableEventTap
+{
+    if (eventsTap) {
+        CGEventTapEnable(eventsTap, true);
+    }
+}
+
 /*
  * Makes the target think all down keys are being released.
  * This prevents a stuck key problem, since we will not see
